# SYNOPSIS

```usage
dd [operand ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`dd` copies its input to its output in blocks, converting the data on the way if asked.
It reads standard input and writes standard output unless told otherwise.
Each <operand> is a word of the form <name>`=`<value>:

: `if=`<file>
  Read <file> instead of standard input.
: `of=`<file>
  Write <file> instead of standard output.
  It is created if it does not exist, mode 0666 less the umask, and truncated unless `seek=` or `conv=notrunc` is given.
: `ibs=`<expr>
  Read blocks of <expr> bytes; the default is 512.
: `obs=`<expr>
  Write blocks of <expr> bytes; the default is 512.
: `bs=`<expr>
  Read and write blocks of <expr> bytes, overriding `ibs=` and `obs=`.
  Unless `conv=` asks for `swab`, `lcase` or `ucase`, each block read is written as it came, so a short read gives a short write.
: `skip=`<n>
  Skip <n> input blocks before copying: by seeking, or by reading them when the input cannot seek.
: `seek=`<n>
  Skip <n> blocks of the output before writing, by seeking; the output is cut at that point unless `conv=notrunc` is given.
: `count=`<n>
  Copy at most <n> input blocks, whole or partial.
: `conv=`<value>`,`...
  Convert as each <value> says:
  `lcase` and `ucase` map ASCII letters to lower or upper case;
  `swab` swaps each pair of bytes;
  `noerror` reports a read error and goes on;
  `notrunc` leaves the output file's length alone;
  `sync` pads each short input block to the input block size with zero bytes.

A block size <expr> is a decimal number, optionally followed by `k` (times 1024) or `b` (times 512); several, joined by `x`, are multiplied.
A size of zero is refused.

When it ends, `dd` writes two lines on standard error: the number of whole and partial blocks read, as `3+1 records in`, and written, as `3+1 records out`.

`dd` is sbase's, and found only in the POSIX namespace: `/boot/bin/posix/dd` is a hard link to `sbase-box`, which runs the command its name names, and `/lib/ns/posix` (a manifest's `ns=posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `dd` finds it there.
There is no native `dd`.

